      "text_raw": "All these rail-to-rail ampli-\nfiers need a double V\nGSn\n+V as a supply voltage\nDSsatn\nto be able to operate.\nFor example, for a V of\nT\n0.6 V and a V −V of\nGS T\n0.15 V, the supply voltage\nbecomes 1.8 V. Reducing\nthe V to 0.3 V as is the\nT\ncase for CMOS technologies\nwith 90 nm channel length\nand below, the supply volt-\nage could reach the 1 V level\nindeed. The condition is\nhowever, that the transistor\nworks more in weak inversion. Its V −V of 0.10 V is very close to the 70–80 mV crossover\nGS T\nvalue between strong and weak inversion (see Chapter 1).",
      "text": "All these rail-to-rail amplifiers need a double V GSn +V as a supply voltage DSsatn to be able to operate. For example, for a V of T 0.6 V and a V −V of GS T 0.15 V, the supply voltage becomes 1.8 V. Reducing the V to 0.3 V as is the T case for CMOS technologies with 90 nm channel length and below, the supply voltage could reach the 1 V level indeed. The condition is however, that the transistor works more in weak inversion. Its V −V of 0.10 V is very close to the 70–80 mV crossover GS T value between strong and weak inversion (see Chapter 1)."
